    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d202">

                <pb facs="0170" n="158" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg158"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">810.20 Defense/34<hi rendition="#superscript"
                        >3/12</hi>: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Peru</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Norweb</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Lima</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-06-14T22:00:00-05:00">June 14, 1940—10
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 11:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>78. A conference was held this afternoon with the Minister of Foreign Affairs,
                    Minister of War,<anchor n="19" xml:id="d202-f1"/> Minister of Marine and
                        Aviation<anchor n="20" xml:id="d202-f2"/> and the Minister of
                        Hacienda.<anchor n="21" xml:id="d202-f3"/> The reaction of the Peruvian
                    officials was definitely favorable. They are meeting with the President this
                    evening and another conference is promised for tomorrow to report the
                    President’s attitude, and further observations.</p>
                <pb facs="0171" n="159" xml:id="pg159"/>
                <p>The Foreign Minister mentioned the necessity of unanimity among the American
                    Republics if the plan is to be effective. This was the only suggestion of a
                    reservation that arose during the conversations.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Norweb</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
